    "created_at": "2019-07-05T13:46:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-07-05T13:46:37Z",
    "body": "I think the natural implementation direction of such a change would be these three steps, one for each of the places where `_self` is mentioned:\r\n\r\n- Extend “valid browsing context name or keyword” to allow an empty string, so that `<base target=\"\">` and `<a target=\"\">` would be valid, and the default value. (This allows esoteric behaviour like `<base target=_self>` paired with `<a target>` on the odd occasion where you might actually prefer the browser’s default behaviour rather than `_self`.)\r\n- Adjust the description of the ordinary effect of the empty string in the table below that.\r\n- Distinguish between `_self` and the empty string in “The rules for choosing a browsing context”.\r\n\r\nThe *spec* never calls `_self` the default, but MDN definitely does, and I expect other sources that talk about targets do; all such sources would then need to be updated to reflect the change (which, as noted, is already reality in Firefox).",
